<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tarim, Yemen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tarim is a town in the Wadi Hadramawt in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, long the religious centre of the valley. It is the seat of Ba 'Alawi sayyid families and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> scholarship, with many mosques and the Dar al-Mustafa school. It was ruled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiyun">Seiyun</a> by the Kathiri sultans.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tarim-yemen.jpg" alt="Tarim, Yemen" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ljuba brank · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:13_Tarim_(10).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Tarim lies in the Hadhramaut valley of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>, about 180 kilometres inland from the coast and 35 kilometres north-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seiyun">Seiyun</a>, whose airport is the nearest. The Hadhramaut spans about 90,000 square kilometres, with a narrow arid coastal plain below a plateau averaging around 1,400 metres in altitude, cut by deep wadis. Tarim has a hot desert climate with very little rain, though a few times a year heavy rainfall brings flooding. It is described as the theological, juridical and academic centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadi-hadhramaut">Wadi Hadhramaut</a>, and as home to a very high concentration of Sayyids, descendants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a>. Imam al-Haddad was among its scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Tradition holds that the citizens of Tarim converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> when a delegation from Hadhramaut met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophet-muhammad">Prophet Muhammad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/medina">Medina</a> in 631, and that Abu Bakr prayed for more scholars and water for the town because its people stood with him in the Ridda wars of 632 to 633. A Wahhabi invasion in 1809 destroyed valuable books and documents from the Robat at Tarim and ravaged the economy, which increased emigration, above all to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>There Umar bin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/awadh">Awadh</a> al-Qu'aiti made a fortune and founded the Qu'aiti dynasty, which signed a treaty with the British in 1888 and created a unified sultanate in 1902, within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aden-protectorate">Aden Protectorate</a>. Tarim stayed under Kathiri rule, as a pocket of Kathiri territory in the country of the Tamim, who owed allegiance to the Qu'aiti sultanate.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In February 1937 the Qu'aiti and Kathiri sultanates made peace, known as Ingrams' Peace after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harold-ingrams">Harold Ingrams</a>, the first political officer in Hadhramaut, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sharif-ul-hashim-of-sulu">Sayyid Abu Bakr</a> al-Kaf, whose family fortune, made in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, paid for it. The British withdrew from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-yemen">South Yemen</a> in November 1967, the National Liberation Front took power, and Tarim became part of the People's Democratic Republic of Yemen until South and North Yemen unified in 1990.</p><p>The town is said to contain up to 365 mosques, one of which, the Sirjis Mosque, dates to the seventh century. The Al-Muhdhar Mosque has a mud minaret of about 53 metres, completed in 1914. The Al-Kaf Library holds more than 5,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, and about thirty mansions were built between the 1870s and 1930s in styles taken from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tarim, Yemen” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kathiri sultans ruled from Seiyun, whose mud-brick palace still dominates the town, and from <b>Tarim</b>, the religious centre of the valley's sayyid families and Shafi'i scholarship.</blockquote><a href="/state/kathiri-sultanate">Kathiri Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
